WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:04.400
These sharks are growing miniature antibodies

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:07.020
First discovered in camels in the 1980s,

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:08.940
these tiny antibodies--about one half

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:12.320
the size of conventional ones -- are also produced by

00:00:12.329 --> 00:00:15.690
sharks, llamas, and a few other animals.

00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:18.760
Regular antibodies are made of four protein chains --

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:21.580
2 heavy, 2 light.

00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:26.120
Small antibodies are made of just 2 heavy chains.

00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:28.120
It’s thought that this super lightweight version

00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:30.480
of antibodies has evolved independently several times

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:34.580
suggesting they are highly effective in these animals.

00:00:34.580 --> 00:00:37.880
We just don’t know exactly what they are doing.

00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:39.680
This hasn’t stopped researchers from using

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:42.560
mini-antibodies, and their useful fragments --

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:44.720
often termed nanobodies --

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:47.900
in clinical and basic science studies.

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:50.380
Nanobodies gained early fame for their role in

00:00:50.380 --> 00:00:54.080
helping to visualize the structure of complex proteins.

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:57.060
Their small size lets them bind tightly and deeply --

00:00:57.060 --> 00:00:59.800
and this can help stabilize ornate molecules whose

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:03.100
flexibility thwarts the imaging process

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:04.880
Their structure also has the advantage of

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:08.240
letting them function inside of a cell.

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:10.280
It’s a new way for researchers to study

00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:13.960
proteins in action--forcing hookups, or blocking them,

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:16.700
or taking targeted molecules out of the game

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:20.180
altogether--as the cell goes about its business.

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:23.160
On the clinical side, researchers are testing nanobodies

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:26.720
and other small antibodies in the fight against diseases

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:29.700
from lupus to lung
fibrosis.

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:32.960
One holdup has been the llamas and the sharks.

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:34.700
In order to get nanobodies tailored

00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:37.320
to a specific molecule, the molecule must

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:39.860
be first injected into the animals.

00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:41.940
The animals then make small antibodies

00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:44.440
over the course of weeks or months.

00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:46.580
Researchers use blood cells from the animals

00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:49.380
to obtain genes for the small antibodies and

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:53.420
then use bacteria to produce the nanobodies in the lab.

00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:56.340
But it might soon be easier to obtain nanobodies--

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:59.700
a team has made a nanobody library in yeast cells

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:01.240
that will allow researchers to

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:04.160
skip the animal-inoculation step.

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:06.200
Next on the horizon for nanobodies--

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:09.280
getting them to stick to tumors for imaging purposes

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:11.300
and using them to allow drugs to cross the

00:02:11.300 --> 00:02:14.420
blood-brain barrier to tackle diseases of the brain.

